1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,685
This is a complete walkthrough of the paper that

2
00:00:03,785 --> 00:00:07,708
invented what we now call L 2 regularization: Ridge

3
00:00:07,808 --> 00:00:13,466
Regression, Biased Estimation for Nonorthogonal Problems, by Arthur Hoerl

4
00:00:13,566 --> 00:00:17,804
and Robert Kennard, published in Technometrics in 1970.

5
00:00:17,866 --> 00:00:22,054
Hoerl worked at the DuPont chemical company, and this method

6
00:00:22,154 --> 00:00:26,485
was born from real industrial data that kept breaking ordinary

7
00:00:26,585 --> 00:00:31,059
regression. Fifty years later, the same idea lives inside almost

8
00:00:31,159 --> 00:00:35,347
every neural network, under the name weight decay. To follow

9
00:00:35,447 --> 00:00:39,420
along, you only need basic linear regression and a little

10
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:43,422
matrix algebra. We will define everything else as we go.

11
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:48,154
Let's set the stage. Imagine predicting a car's fuel

12
00:00:48,254 --> 00:00:53,222
consumption from three measurements: its weight, its engine size,

13
00:00:53,322 --> 00:00:57,745
and its horsepower. That is multiple linear regression. We

14
00:00:57,845 --> 00:01:01,332
observe n outcomes, collected in the vector Y.

15
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:05,173
The matrix X holds the data: one row per car, one

16
00:01:05,273 --> 00:01:10,390
column per predictor. Beta is the vector of p unknown coefficients

17
00:01:10,490 --> 00:01:15,844
we want to estimate: how much each predictor contributes. And epsilon

18
00:01:15,944 --> 00:01:21,220
is random noise, with mean zero and constant variance sigma squared.

19
00:01:21,266 --> 00:01:25,285
One more thing about our example. A heavy car usually has

20
00:01:25,385 --> 00:01:29,694
a big engine, and a big engine usually means more horsepower.

21
00:01:29,794 --> 00:01:34,391
The three predictors are correlated with each other. Keep that in

22
00:01:34,491 --> 00:01:39,667
mind, because correlated predictors are the villain of this entire paper.

23
00:01:39,767 --> 00:01:44,364
The paper also assumes the predictors are standardized, so that X

24
00:01:44,464 --> 00:01:46,966
transpose X is a correlation matrix.

25
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:52,501
How do we estimate beta? The classical recipe is least squares:

26
00:01:52,601 --> 00:01:56,998
choose the beta that makes the residual sum of squares, the

27
00:01:57,098 --> 00:02:02,868
total squared gap between predictions and observations, as small as possible.

28
00:02:02,933 --> 00:02:07,159
This is a smooth function of beta, so we minimize it the

29
00:02:07,259 --> 00:02:12,026
calculus way: take the gradient and set it to zero. That yields

30
00:02:12,126 --> 00:02:17,975
the so called normal equations. Solving them gives the famous closed formula:

31
00:02:18,075 --> 00:02:22,842
beta hat equals the inverse of X transpose X, times X transpose

32
00:02:22,942 --> 00:02:26,473
Y. This is equation one point one in the paper.

33
00:02:26,533 --> 00:02:31,687
A quick definition. An estimator is unbiased if, over many repeated

34
00:02:31,787 --> 00:02:37,255
experiments, its average lands exactly on the true value. Least squares

35
00:02:37,355 --> 00:02:42,039
is unbiased, and the Gauss Markov theorem says that among all

36
00:02:42,139 --> 00:02:47,372
linear unbiased estimators, it has the smallest variance. For half a

37
00:02:47,472 --> 00:02:51,921
century, that theorem made least squares feel untouchable.

38
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,720
But look closely: the formula must invert X transpose X. Hoerl and

39
00:02:56,820 --> 00:03:01,541
Kennard's opening move is to ask what happens to that inverse when

40
00:03:01,641 --> 00:03:07,020
the predictors are correlated. That inverse is where everything goes wrong.

41
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:11,818
Before we meet the paper's main idea, we need one concept:

42
00:03:11,918 --> 00:03:17,072
the difference between being unbiased and being accurate. Picture two dart

43
00:03:17,172 --> 00:03:21,404
players aiming at a bullseye, which represents the true beta.

44
00:03:21,466 --> 00:03:26,042
The first player is unbiased: their darts are centered on the

45
00:03:26,142 --> 00:03:30,719
bullseye on average. But the spread is huge. Any single dart,

46
00:03:30,819 --> 00:03:34,398
any single dataset, can land far from the truth.

47
00:03:34,466 --> 00:03:39,919
The second player aims slightly off center, a deliberate bias, but the darts

48
00:03:40,019 --> 00:03:44,960
land in a tight cluster. Every single throw is close to the bullseye.

49
00:03:45,060 --> 00:03:49,270
If you had to bet on one throw, you would pick this player.

50
00:03:49,333 --> 00:03:55,142
Statisticians score this game with the mean squared error, which splits exactly

51
00:03:55,242 --> 00:04:00,304
into squared bias plus variance. Least squares fixes the bias at zero

52
00:04:00,404 --> 00:04:05,615
and hopes the variance is small. Hoerl and Kennard's whole argument is:

53
00:04:05,715 --> 00:04:10,926
when predictors are correlated, that is a terrible deal, and you should

54
00:04:11,026 --> 00:04:14,217
trade a little bias for a lot less variance.

55
00:04:15,017 --> 00:04:19,654
Now let's see what correlation looks like geometrically. Here are

56
00:04:19,754 --> 00:04:24,755
two predictors, say weight and horsepower, plotted against each other.

57
00:04:24,855 --> 00:04:29,055
Because they are strongly correlated, the data cloud is not

58
00:04:29,155 --> 00:04:32,845
round. It is squeezed into a narrow, tilted ellipse.

59
00:04:32,884 --> 00:04:37,416
The eigenvalues of X transpose X measure exactly this shape.

60
00:04:37,516 --> 00:04:41,817
Each eigenvalue tells you how much the data spreads along

61
00:04:41,917 --> 00:04:46,141
one principal direction. The long green axis has a large

62
00:04:46,241 --> 00:04:49,848
eigenvalue: lots of spread, lots of information.

63
00:04:49,884 --> 00:04:54,739
But the short red axis has a tiny eigenvalue. Along that direction

64
00:04:54,839 --> 00:04:59,320
the data barely varies at all, so the data contains almost no

65
00:04:59,420 --> 00:05:05,477
information about that combination of coefficients. This is what the paper's title

66
00:05:05,577 --> 00:05:10,809
means by a nonorthogonal problem: X transpose X is nearly singular, and

67
00:05:10,909 --> 00:05:14,864
inverting it means dividing by these tiny eigenvalues.

68
00:05:15,684 --> 00:05:20,178
The paper measures the damage with one clean formula. The expected

69
00:05:20,278 --> 00:05:24,981
squared distance between the least squares estimate and the true beta

70
00:05:25,081 --> 00:05:29,366
equals sigma squared times the sum of one over each eigenvalue.

71
00:05:29,466 --> 00:05:33,056
This is equations one point five and one point eight.

72
00:05:33,117 --> 00:05:38,063
Read it as a warning. With orthogonal predictors every eigenvalue is

73
00:05:38,163 --> 00:05:41,847
one, and the error is a modest p sigma squared. But

74
00:05:41,947 --> 00:05:47,264
with strong correlation, the smallest eigenvalue might be zero point zero

75
00:05:47,364 --> 00:05:51,642
zero two, and then that single term multiplies the noise by

76
00:05:51,742 --> 00:05:56,465
five hundred. On average, the estimate is nowhere near the truth.

77
00:05:56,517 --> 00:06:00,730
There is a second symptom, equation one point five A. The

78
00:06:00,830 --> 00:06:06,027
expected squared length of the estimated vector equals the true length

79
00:06:06,127 --> 00:06:11,475
plus that same inflated sum. So the coefficients come out systematically

80
00:06:11,575 --> 00:06:15,939
too large, and, as the authors put it in their conclusions,

81
00:06:16,039 --> 00:06:20,176
some may even have the wrong sign. An engineer reads the

82
00:06:20,276 --> 00:06:23,657
fitted model and it contradicts basic physics.

83
00:06:24,450 --> 00:06:29,646
Let's watch this happen with real numbers. I generated two predictors with

84
00:06:29,746 --> 00:06:34,799
correlation zero point nine nine eight, set the true coefficients to one

85
00:06:34,899 --> 00:06:39,236
and two, and fitted the same model on three different draws of

86
00:06:39,336 --> 00:06:44,174
the noise. Only the noise changes. Watch the least squares estimates.

87
00:06:44,250 --> 00:06:49,821
First draw: minus zero point eight and plus three point five. Second draw:

88
00:06:49,921 --> 00:06:55,338
plus ten point three and minus seven point nine. Third: plus eight point

89
00:06:55,438 --> 00:06:59,936
five and minus five point six. The truth is one and two. The

90
00:07:00,036 --> 00:07:05,300
estimates are enormous, they flip sign from draw to draw, and they are

91
00:07:05,400 --> 00:07:10,358
pure noise. This is exactly the instability the formula predicted.

92
00:07:10,450 --> 00:07:15,741
Now the ridge estimates on the very same data. One point one and one

93
00:07:15,841 --> 00:07:21,133
point two. One point one and one point zero. One point three and one

94
00:07:21,233 --> 00:07:26,286
point two. Stable across all three draws, and close to the truth.

95
00:07:26,384 --> 00:07:31,060
Notice something honest here: ridge gives roughly one point one for

96
00:07:31,160 --> 00:07:35,195
both coefficients, while the truth is one and two. The two

97
00:07:35,295 --> 00:07:40,114
predictors are so correlated that the data genuinely cannot tell them

98
00:07:40,214 --> 00:07:44,320
apart, so ridge splits the credit between them. That is the

99
00:07:44,420 --> 00:07:48,242
bias. But it is a small, predictable error instead of a

100
00:07:48,342 --> 00:07:52,876
catastrophic random one. That is the trade this paper is selling.

101
00:07:53,617 --> 00:07:58,172
Here is the paper's remedy, equation two point one, and it

102
00:07:58,272 --> 00:08:03,149
is one line. Before inverting, add a small positive constant k

103
00:08:03,249 --> 00:08:07,724
to every diagonal element of X transpose X. The result is

104
00:08:07,824 --> 00:08:12,862
the ridge estimator. Hoerl had first proposed this back in 1962,

105
00:08:12,962 --> 00:08:17,999
and the name comes from ridge analysis, a technique for studying

106
00:08:18,099 --> 00:08:22,173
quadratic response surfaces in chemical engineering.

107
00:08:22,217 --> 00:08:27,014
Why does this fix things? Adding k times the identity shifts

108
00:08:27,114 --> 00:08:32,482
every eigenvalue up by exactly k. The dangerous reciprocal one over

109
00:08:32,582 --> 00:08:37,053
lambda becomes one over lambda plus k. Even if lambda is

110
00:08:37,153 --> 00:08:41,786
nearly zero, the denominator is at least k, so nothing can

111
00:08:41,886 --> 00:08:45,949
explode anymore. The noise amplification is capped.

112
00:08:46,017 --> 00:08:50,088
The paper also shows, in equations two point four and two

113
00:08:50,188 --> 00:08:54,405
point eight, that the ridge estimate is just a fixed matrix

114
00:08:54,505 --> 00:08:58,430
Z applied to the least squares estimate, and that it is

115
00:08:58,530 --> 00:09:03,552
always strictly shorter. So ridge does not compute something alien: it

116
00:09:03,652 --> 00:09:08,162
takes the least squares answer and shrinks it toward zero, with

117
00:09:08,262 --> 00:09:12,333
k controlling how hard. At k equals zero we recover least

118
00:09:12,433 --> 00:09:17,309
squares exactly; as k grows to infinity, everything shrinks to zero.

119
00:09:18,050 --> 00:09:23,219
The paper gives a beautiful geometric picture, in section three. The blue

120
00:09:23,319 --> 00:09:28,127
ellipses are contours of the residual sum of squares: every point on

121
00:09:28,227 --> 00:09:33,180
one ellipse explains the data equally well. Their common center is the

122
00:09:33,280 --> 00:09:38,305
least squares solution, the bottom of the valley. Note the ellipses are

123
00:09:38,405 --> 00:09:42,852
long and thin: that is the small eigenvalue again. You can walk

124
00:09:42,952 --> 00:09:46,894
far along the valley floor while the fit barely changes.

125
00:09:46,984 --> 00:09:52,904
Now the paper's key characterization. Among all coefficient vectors that give the

126
00:09:53,004 --> 00:09:57,809
same residual error, that is, all points on the same blue contour,

127
00:09:57,909 --> 00:10:02,640
the ridge estimate is the one closest to the origin: the shortest

128
00:10:02,740 --> 00:10:08,289
vector. The authors derive it with a Lagrange multiplier, and the multiplier

129
00:10:08,389 --> 00:10:12,302
turns out to be one over k. In plain words: if several

130
00:10:12,402 --> 00:10:17,580
explanations fit the data equally well, take the most conservative one.

131
00:10:17,650 --> 00:10:21,464
Here is that solution. The green circle shows all vectors

132
00:10:21,564 --> 00:10:24,761
of the same length as the ridge estimate, and it

133
00:10:24,861 --> 00:10:28,263
is exactly tangent to the blue contour at the ridge

134
00:10:28,363 --> 00:10:32,932
point. Tangency is the fingerprint of a constrained optimum: neither

135
00:10:33,032 --> 00:10:36,847
curve crosses the other, so you cannot fit better without

136
00:10:36,947 --> 00:10:41,310
getting longer, and you cannot get shorter without fitting worse.

137
00:10:41,384 --> 00:10:46,006
And as k sweeps from zero to infinity, the ridge solution traces

138
00:10:46,106 --> 00:10:50,580
this yellow path from the least squares point all the way down

139
00:10:50,680 --> 00:10:54,565
to the origin. Every value of k picks one point on the

140
00:10:54,665 --> 00:10:59,140
path. Choosing k well is the subject of the next two sections.

141
00:10:59,917 --> 00:11:04,552
Now the mathematical heart of the paper, section four, and

142
00:11:04,652 --> 00:11:09,042
its famous Figure one, which we recreate here with real

143
00:11:09,142 --> 00:11:14,266
computed curves. Equation four point five splits the total error

144
00:11:14,366 --> 00:11:18,593
of the ridge estimator into two parts: gamma one, the

145
00:11:18,693 --> 00:11:22,593
total variance, plus gamma two, the squared bias.

146
00:11:22,684 --> 00:11:26,879
The variance, in blue, starts at the inflated least

147
00:11:26,979 --> 00:11:31,511
squares value and, by theorem four point one, decreases

148
00:11:31,611 --> 00:11:36,312
monotonically as k grows. Shrinking suppresses the noise.

149
00:11:36,384 --> 00:11:40,230
The squared bias, in red, starts at exactly zero

150
00:11:40,330 --> 00:11:45,657
and, by theorem four point two, increases monotonically. Shrinking

151
00:11:45,757 --> 00:11:49,604
too hard drags the estimate away from the truth.

152
00:11:49,684 --> 00:11:54,863
Now add them. And here is the crucial asymmetry, computed in equations

153
00:11:54,963 --> 00:11:59,162
four point nine and four point ten. At k equals zero, the

154
00:11:59,262 --> 00:12:04,441
bias curve is perfectly flat, its derivative is zero, but the variance

155
00:12:04,541 --> 00:12:09,721
curve plunges with a steep negative slope. So the total must initially

156
00:12:09,821 --> 00:12:15,302
fall below the least squares level. Near zero, variance reduction is free:

157
00:12:15,402 --> 00:12:18,168
the bias has not started charging yet.

158
00:12:18,217 --> 00:12:23,260
This is theorem four point three, the existence theorem. There always

159
00:12:23,360 --> 00:12:27,807
exists a positive k for which ridge has strictly smaller mean

160
00:12:27,907 --> 00:12:32,802
squared error than least squares. The paper even gives a sufficient

161
00:12:32,902 --> 00:12:38,094
condition: any k below sigma squared over the largest squared component

162
00:12:38,194 --> 00:12:43,536
of beta works. Read that again: the celebrated optimal unbiased estimator

163
00:12:43,636 --> 00:12:48,381
is never actually optimal. Some amount of shrinkage always helps.

164
00:12:49,117 --> 00:12:54,673
One practical question remains: how do you choose k? The paper's answer is

165
00:12:54,773 --> 00:13:00,176
a diagnostic plot called the ridge trace. This one is computed from real

166
00:13:00,276 --> 00:13:05,909
data: four predictors, where the first two are almost copies of each other,

167
00:13:06,009 --> 00:13:11,260
and the true coefficients are one point five, one point five, one, and

168
00:13:11,360 --> 00:13:16,075
minus one. We solve the ridge equation for every k from zero to

169
00:13:16,175 --> 00:13:19,209
one and plot each coefficient as a curve.

170
00:13:19,250 --> 00:13:23,829
Look at k equals zero, which is plain least squares. The red

171
00:13:23,929 --> 00:13:29,678
coefficient comes out negative, minus zero point five five, even though its

172
00:13:29,778 --> 00:13:34,435
true value is plus one point five. The wrong sign, exactly as

173
00:13:34,535 --> 00:13:39,894
the paper warned. Its correlated twin, in blue, grabbed all the credit

174
00:13:39,994 --> 00:13:44,885
and overshot to three point five. As k increases, the two curves

175
00:13:44,985 --> 00:13:48,238
quickly converge and settle near the truth.

176
00:13:48,317 --> 00:13:53,394
Section seven gives four criteria for reading this plot. Choose the

177
00:13:53,494 --> 00:13:58,029
k at which, first, the system stabilizes and behaves like an

178
00:13:58,129 --> 00:14:05,215
orthogonal one. Second, the coefficients have reasonable absolute values. Third, coefficients

179
00:14:05,315 --> 00:14:10,932
with apparently wrong signs at zero have corrected themselves. And fourth,

180
00:14:11,032 --> 00:14:16,264
the residual sum of squares has not been inflated unreasonably. Here,

181
00:14:16,364 --> 00:14:20,899
around k equals zero point three, all four are satisfied. It

182
00:14:20,999 --> 00:14:25,922
is a graphical, judgment based choice; today we would automate it

183
00:14:26,022 --> 00:14:29,785
with cross validation, but the logic is unchanged.

184
00:14:30,584 --> 00:14:35,591
Let's zoom out and collect what this paper established. First, the

185
00:14:35,691 --> 00:14:41,318
philosophy: unbiased does not mean accurate, because error is bias squared

186
00:14:41,418 --> 00:14:46,193
plus variance, and least squares puts everything on one side of

187
00:14:46,293 --> 00:14:52,849
that ledger. Second, the diagnosis: correlated predictors create tiny eigenvalues, and

188
00:14:52,949 --> 00:14:57,028
tiny eigenvalues make least squares estimates explode.

189
00:14:57,117 --> 00:15:01,957
Third, the cure: add k to the diagonal before inverting, which

190
00:15:02,057 --> 00:15:07,853
shrinks the solution toward zero. And fourth, the guarantee: some positive

191
00:15:07,953 --> 00:15:12,713
k always gives a strictly better estimate than least squares.

192
00:15:12,784 --> 00:15:17,650
The paper itself, in section six, connects ridge to two other big

193
00:15:17,750 --> 00:15:22,922
ideas. It is equivalent to putting a Bayesian prior on beta, centered

194
00:15:23,022 --> 00:15:27,277
at zero. And it is a cousin of Stein shrinkage, the other

195
00:15:27,377 --> 00:15:33,084
great sixties result showing that shrinking always helps in high dimensions.

196
00:15:33,150 --> 00:15:38,609
Today the idea is everywhere. Penalizing the squared length of the parameters

197
00:15:38,709 --> 00:15:43,736
is called L 2 regularization, and in deep learning, weight decay: every

198
00:15:43,836 --> 00:15:49,439
neural network trained with weight decay is running Hoerl and Kennard's recipe.

199
00:15:49,539 --> 00:15:54,782
Twenty six years after this paper, Robert Tibshirani asked what happens if

200
00:15:54,882 --> 00:15:59,908
you penalize the absolute values instead of the squares. That gives the

201
00:16:00,008 --> 00:16:04,529
lasso, which can set coefficients exactly to zero, and it is the

202
00:16:04,629 --> 00:16:07,922
subject of the next video. Thanks for watching.

